From e7d693f557d225b542436de75651d6602013c344 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matt C <latentcollapse@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 04:30:23 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07/42] RLM spike: propagate baseToolsOverride to spawned
 children

Neither the inline (_createInlineRlmSubagentRuntime) nor daemon-mode
(admitRlmSubagentRuntime) child-construction path forwarded the
parent's baseToolsOverride, so every RLM child unconditionally built
a real ipython kernel regardless of the parent's substrate mode. Worse:
since the parent's active tool names are inherited but don't resolve
against the child's (different) tool registry, a NEURA-only parent's
child silently ended up with zero active tools, not a fallback to
ipython (setActiveToolsByName drops unknown names silently).

Threads baseToolsOverride through CreateRlmSubagentRuntimeOptions,
_createRlmSubagentRuntimeOptions, and both child-construction paths.
Safe to share tool instances across parent/child: neurabash.ts's tool
is stateless per call with globally-scoped (not session-scoped) depot
dirs. Regression test added and verified against stashed pre-fix
source (fails before, passes after).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
